# Technical writing

Use these rules when writing or editing Markdown.

- Clarity over cleverness. Simple words. Short sentences. One idea at a time.
- Write to what the reader knows and needs to do.
- Use active voice. Make responsibility and action explicit.
- Be concrete. Prefer specific, observable outcomes over abstract claims.
- Strong structure: clear headers, short sections, scannable layout.
- Front-load key info. Say what it is, why it matters, and when to use it first.
- One term per concept. Do not rename it later.
- Introduce concepts once. Avoid forward references.
- Show the happy path with a minimal example before theory.
- State assumptions, limits, and failure modes.
- Remove anything that does not add meaning.
